Devin, you asked what rate to use for the euro-denominated instrument and I w=
ant to answer it properly rather than quickly, because this is the sort of th=
ing that quietly corrupts a model.

There are three candidates in play: the rate on the instrument date, the rate=
 on the measurement date named in the fund's conventions memorandum, and what=
ever rate the intern's working file picked up from a spreadsheet. Only one of=
 those is the policy answer.

Kip's note from the working file is below, reproduced as written. I am includ=
ing it because it shows how easy it is to pick up the wrong one.

Working figure carried forward from the earlier thread: 1.0900. A treasury-te=
am email quotes an FX rate for the EUR SAFE conversion that does not match th=
e governing source. Please reconcile against the source-of-record instrument =
and its effective date before this reaches a model.

The policy answer is the one in the conventions memorandum, and the memorandu=
m names the measurement date explicitly. Do not substitute a date that happen=
s to be more convenient, and do not average.

Kip, this is not a criticism. The working file did exactly what a working fil=
e does. It just cannot be the source for the deliverable.

